00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:03.290
A quick review of what we are talking about
in terms of the API's being affected . These

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:06.480
are the E-utilities only.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:09.810
No blast APIs, no other APIs are affected.

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:16.290
The E-utilities are the Entrez APIs esummary,
efetch, elink and epost.

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:21.850
These are things you use to do searches in
PubMed, or gene or nucleotide, to get esummary

00:04:21.850 --> 00:04:29.460
doc sums, fasta, abstracts, citation formats,
and various types of literature and sequence

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:30.630
data.

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:32.850
Those of the APIs you need to be concerned
about.

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:38.770
If you are using them, then you need to pay
attention, if using blast APIs, it's not something

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:40.460
relevant at the moment.

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:41.760
What are keys?

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:44.140
A API keys is a unique string.

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:47.470
A string that will be added to the URL request.

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:51.390
The string identifies you to the API.

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:55.560
It needs to be included in every API request
you make.

00:04:55.560 --> 00:05:03.160
These API keys are attached to a NCBI account
. Some of you may know as a My NCBI account.

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:09.340
But one account can have a single API key.

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:14.470
One of the big reasons we are doing this is
we want the API to be more stable and reliable

00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:15.550
to you.

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:22.980
And having these keys helps us make that happen,
by being better able to monitor and regulate

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:26.190
the amount of traffic that comes into the
services.

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:28.880
We would love to have infinite capacity and
we don't.

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:30.140
No one does.

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:35.620
But to make it seem as this we have capacity
to meet your needs, this is a way for us to

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:41.300
help regulate that, to help you regulate it
to the point it can work for you as well as

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:42.300
possible.

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:44.260
So how do you get a key?

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:49.960
A reminder, you go to my NCBI , or a NCBI
account.

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:56.240
Find the link in the upper right-hand corner
of the NCBI pages.

00:05:56.240 --> 00:06:00.890
And sign in . You can create an account if
you don't already have one.

00:06:00.890 --> 00:06:04.520
If you have an account, please use that.

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:08.470
If you'd like to create a new account for
software, that is fine.

00:06:08.470 --> 00:06:14.170
There is a button under API key management
where you can create an API key.

00:06:14.170 --> 00:06:17.930
It will put it on the screen, copy it.

00:06:17.930 --> 00:06:21.280
How do you use the key?

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:22.280
Very simple.

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:25.890
You assign it to the parameter api_key.

00:06:25.890 --> 00:06:35.930
You put that parameter in any, all of your
E-utilities requests . I'm showing you the

00:06:35.930 --> 00:06:40.310
einfo, and esearch for example, put api_key
equals and whatever the key is.

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:48.240
If you are an EDirect user, there is an environmental
variable named NCBI_API_KEY.

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:58.120
EDirect will automatically attach your API
key to all outgoing requests.

00:06:58.120 --> 00:07:02.080
If you want to get a new key you can, but
the old key dies immediately.

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:10.260
You can only have one key per NCBI account
. If you think somebody else has got it and

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:16.900
you are concerned about it, whatever reason,
you're tired of it you can get rid of it.

00:07:16.900 --> 00:07:22.180
When you replacement it the other one goes
away immediately, no longer active.

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:26.270
I want to say a word before I start talking
about rates.

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:27.910
How we define rates.

00:07:27.910 --> 00:07:33.240
What we will be talking about is key setting
rate limits on the number of requests coming

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:34.810
in.

00:07:34.810 --> 00:07:40.370
If you get an error, about the rates, I am
showing at the top of the slide, and error

00:07:40.370 --> 00:07:45.761
text saying the API rate limit is exceeded,
and a count meaning the number of requests

00:07:45.761 --> 00:07:48.970
that we saw in a particular second.

00:07:48.970 --> 00:07:54.190
The important thing for people to realize,
we are looking at a one second window and

00:07:54.190 --> 00:08:01.090
we care about when the requests were received
at NCBI, not execution time . What we really

00:08:01.090 --> 00:08:06.449
care about is the fact that you started more
than your limit of requests in a one second

00:08:06.449 --> 00:08:07.620
window.

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:11.980
In the bottom you see a lot of requests that
are taking a number of seconds.

00:08:11.980 --> 00:08:17.130
But there is no time during that activity
where more than three request are being received

00:08:17.130 --> 00:08:18.130
per second.

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:22.080
For example if your limit was three.

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:24.169
That's one of the default limits.

00:08:24.169 --> 00:08:26.550
That's how the rates are being defined.

00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:30.570
There is a new sandbox that we have.

00:08:30.570 --> 00:08:35.700
The new sandbox is an environment where the
keys will be active permanently.

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:38.640
And so this is a great place to go to test.

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:39.920
You can hit this.

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:47.690
What is different about this for those familiar
with the API, the URL is api dot NCBI dot

00:08:47.690 --> 00:08:50.300
NLM dot gov/eutils.

00:08:50.300 --> 00:09:04.230
Not www or eutils dot ncbi.

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:06.650
The keys will always be active there.

00:09:06.650 --> 00:09:08.880
This will simulate production.

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:12.550
It is active now and you can start testing
now.

00:09:12.550 --> 00:09:17.140
At the bottom of the slide I've put example
URLs.

00:09:17.140 --> 00:09:23.280
If I want to do a esearch I just do api.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/eutils.fcgi?

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:34.740
then whatever you are doing just change the
base URL to API dot NCBI slash eutils.

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:37.130
What happens in the sandbox.

00:09:37.130 --> 00:09:44.160
If you've been it one of the previous webinars
you will have heard this.

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:49.220
This is the behavior that happens now in the
sandbox and will happen when we go live in

00:09:49.220 --> 00:09:50.910
full production.

00:09:50.910 --> 00:09:53.779
If you don't have a key, let's start there.

00:09:53.779 --> 00:09:59.480
If you don't have a key the limit is three
requests per second for each individual IP.

00:09:59.480 --> 00:10:03.250
What I am showing is three IP addresses.

00:10:03.250 --> 00:10:08.260
The first one two requests per second, that
is fine, no key.

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:13.190
The second one has five requests per second,
that would cause an error.

00:10:13.190 --> 00:10:16.370
You will get an error when you get those back.

00:10:16.370 --> 00:10:18.540
And one and a half request per second, no
problem.

00:10:18.540 --> 00:10:25.230
As long as you are below 3 requests per second
for any 1 second moving window, you are fine.

00:10:25.230 --> 00:10:29.279
What happens to those requests that receive
an error, they are not processed.

00:10:29.279 --> 00:10:33.330
You are not blocked, there's nothing like
that happening.

00:10:33.330 --> 00:10:39.210
Once the second window passes and you go beneath
the rate limit, you will get access again,

00:10:39.210 --> 00:10:40.210
immediately.

00:10:40.210 --> 00:10:41.320
The requests will start working.

00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:43.990
What you will see in your data is gaps.

00:10:43.990 --> 00:10:46.399
Where you're not getting dated back.

00:10:46.399 --> 00:10:48.460
Why am I not getting data back?

00:10:48.460 --> 00:10:54.990
Because you are momentarily you are blocked,
if I want to say it that way, by the rate

00:10:54.990 --> 00:10:55.990
limit.

00:10:55.990 --> 00:10:59.720
Keep in mind this is not like the IP getting
blocked or anything like that.

00:10:59.720 --> 00:11:07.470
It is instantaneous, once the rates go below
the limit, you are live and well.

00:11:07.470 --> 00:11:11.980
What happens if you have an IP that two people
are sharing, or multiple people share.

00:11:11.980 --> 00:11:15.490
What happens here is we have two people sharing
the same IP.

00:11:15.490 --> 00:11:20.870
One is doing two request per second and one
is doing five, both get affected.

00:11:20.870 --> 00:11:22.610
Because it is by the IP.

00:11:22.610 --> 00:11:28.620
You have multiple users in the same IP, and
one actor over three request per second, everyone

00:11:28.620 --> 00:11:30.620
is affected.

00:11:30.620 --> 00:11:32.770
That could be a potential issue.

00:11:32.770 --> 00:11:38.000
If you have a lot of people behind a firewall,
whatever it might be.

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:42.029
It depends on the IP, if there is no key.

00:11:42.029 --> 00:11:44.339
That's important to remember.

00:11:44.339 --> 00:11:46.670
If you have a key.

00:11:46.670 --> 00:11:50.620
Now I have several scenarios where there is
a key present.

00:11:50.620 --> 00:11:55.620
In this case the first two people have the
same IP address, but two different keys.

00:11:55.620 --> 00:11:58.300
The first guy has this key, AB23.

00:11:58.300 --> 00:12:01.580
He is under 10 requests per second.

00:12:01.580 --> 00:12:06.360
With the key the default limit goes from 3
to 10.

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:10.589
As long as you don't exceed 10 request per
second with the key, you are fine.

00:12:10.589 --> 00:12:13.140
So seven is cool.

00:12:13.140 --> 00:12:15.500
His friend is doing 15 requests per second.

00:12:15.500 --> 00:12:16.920
He will get errors.

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:18.700
A different key.

00:12:18.700 --> 00:12:23.650
And the third guy at a different IP address,
it doesn't matter.

00:12:23.650 --> 00:12:26.490
Four request per second, he has a different
key.

00:12:26.490 --> 00:12:30.920
My point here is it's about the key, not the
IP address.

00:12:30.920 --> 00:12:36.990
It doesn't matter how many people share the
IP address, it only matters what key you have

00:12:36.990 --> 00:12:40.399
and how many requests are coming in on that
particular key.

00:12:40.399 --> 00:12:45.350
For example, here we have a case where we
are mixing.

00:12:45.350 --> 00:12:47.900
Some people have a key and some people don't
have a key.

00:12:47.900 --> 00:12:48.900
What happens?

00:12:48.900 --> 00:12:50.990
Everyone is on the same IP address.

00:12:50.990 --> 00:12:55.930
The first guy has a key.

00:12:55.930 --> 00:12:57.990
Less than 10, he is fine.

00:12:57.990 --> 00:13:02.650
The second guy has a different key. he's at
15, he gets errors.

00:13:02.650 --> 00:13:07.010
And the third guy no key.

00:13:07.010 --> 00:13:10.140
It looks at the IP.

00:13:10.140 --> 00:13:18.310
That IP is getting seven, +15, equals 22 plus
2 equals 24 request per second, so he gets

00:13:18.310 --> 00:13:19.310
errors.

00:13:19.310 --> 00:13:23.350
This hopefully illustrates to you the problems
you can have if you have a lot of people using

00:13:23.350 --> 00:13:25.209
the same IP.

00:13:25.209 --> 00:13:29.380
The best approach is each person having their
own key.

00:13:29.380 --> 00:13:31.339
That key determines the rate.

00:13:31.339 --> 00:13:40.230
If you have people using the same key we see
the sum of the rate from that same key.

00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:46.360
One person may get errors, and if they get
errors, everyone using that key will get errors.

00:13:46.360 --> 00:13:48.540
It is attached to the key.

00:13:48.540 --> 00:13:54.330
That is an issue if you're using a distributed
scenario where multiple people are using the

00:13:54.330 --> 00:13:55.330
same key.

00:13:55.330 --> 00:14:01.899
If one of them gets above the threshold, then
they all have errors.

00:14:01.899 --> 00:14:09.240
To review quickly, the situation is if you
don't have a key, any IP address that posts

00:14:09.240 --> 00:14:11.269
more than the limit will be affected, they
will get errors.

00:14:11.269 --> 00:14:14.410
It is about IP if there is no key.

00:14:14.410 --> 00:14:16.899
If there is a key, it is about the key.

00:14:16.899 --> 00:14:22.529
So any key that posts more than 10 or whatever
the limit is, will receive errors.

00:14:22.529 --> 00:14:24.610
That is the real bottom line.

00:14:24.610 --> 00:14:28.570
If you don't have a key ts about the IP address,
if you do have a key it's about whoever is

00:14:28.570 --> 00:14:31.570
using that key however many people are using
that key .

00:14:31.570 --> 00:14:35.670
That is what's going to be happening in the
sandbox.

00:14:35.670 --> 00:14:38.490
That happens now, so you can begin testing
that now.

00:14:38.490 --> 00:14:43.300
If you have code you want to test, shoot it
to us and see what happens.

00:14:43.300 --> 00:14:45.450
Verify it is working.

00:14:45.450 --> 00:14:48.920
The testing periods will happen in production.

00:14:48.920 --> 00:14:53.640
We don't yet have a schedule of these but
I will be publicizing them on utilities-announce

00:14:53.640 --> 00:14:55.580
and on social media.

00:14:55.580 --> 00:14:58.640
If you haven't signed up for utilities-announce,
please do.

00:14:58.640 --> 00:15:03.720
I will have a link at the end of the presentation
that will give you access.

00:15:03.720 --> 00:15:08.360
Pay attention to the twitter feed, the blog,
we will talk about it there.

00:15:08.360 --> 00:15:13.050
And the behavior of production during the
test periods will be identical to the sandbox

00:15:13.050 --> 00:15:14.790
now.

00:15:14.790 --> 00:15:20.279
This is a way of checking production code,
also a way for us to see what breaks.

00:15:20.279 --> 00:15:21.279
Right.

00:15:21.279 --> 00:15:26.529
A brief period where we can make sure we are
aware of things, you are aware of things that

00:15:26.529 --> 00:15:28.470
are affected by this.

00:15:28.470 --> 00:15:33.311
We will give you warning about when these
will be happening so you can plan for it.

00:15:33.311 --> 00:15:38.110
It will be no more than an hour and will occur
at different times in the day.

00:15:38.110 --> 00:15:45.930
People use the api 24 seven, we want to make
sure we are getting everybody.

00:15:45.930 --> 00:15:47.930
Full release.

00:15:47.930 --> 00:15:52.480
After we do a few of these testing periods
