00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:06.839
That means all keys will become active.

00:16:06.839 --> 00:16:12.100
We would like to also advise you, we have
a couple of questions about this we have gotten.

00:16:12.100 --> 00:16:16.089
Please continue to use tool an email if you're
using them.

00:16:16.089 --> 00:16:18.770
If you are not using them please begin using
them.

00:16:18.770 --> 00:16:21.460
It does not affect your key in any way.

00:16:21.460 --> 00:16:28.149
Completely separate from the key, however
they help us help you by identifying the software

00:16:28.149 --> 00:16:31.740
package that is attached to the request.

00:16:31.740 --> 00:16:37.899
A number of you have expressed interest in
having distributed keys so one software package

00:16:37.899 --> 00:16:39.829
may have multiple keys.

00:16:39.829 --> 00:16:44.339
The key itself will not identify the software
package, &amp;tool does.

00:16:44.339 --> 00:16:50.820
So if you need us to help you understand activity
with software, and what we are seeing, then

00:16:50.820 --> 00:16:52.350
&amp;tool is required.

00:16:52.350 --> 00:16:59.730
And then &amp;email is the contact information
for the developer , so we can contact you

00:16:59.730 --> 00:17:01.730
about that.

00:17:01.730 --> 00:17:03.870
It should not be a customer contact.

00:17:03.870 --> 00:17:09.470
It should be a developer contact so we can
talk directly to whoever is monitoring it

00:17:09.470 --> 00:17:11.260
and presiding over the code base.

00:17:11.260 --> 00:17:16.850
So we can help you deliver quality services
to your users.

00:17:16.850 --> 00:17:21.020
So, that is basically what I want to say today.

00:17:21.020 --> 00:17:25.669
I want to encourage you to stay tuned on the
social media.

00:17:25.669 --> 00:17:29.059
There is the Learn page if we do give more
webinars.

00:17:29.059 --> 00:17:33.059
We don't have more plans now but if we have
to give additional ones we will publicize

00:17:33.059 --> 00:17:34.200
them there.

00:17:34.200 --> 00:17:35.419
Also on social media.

00:17:35.419 --> 00:17:39.820
And the link I was promising, please subscribe
to utilities-announce.

00:17:39.820 --> 00:17:44.470
When you get the slides you can just click
there and go to the link.

00:17:44.470 --> 00:17:47.399
It's the www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov slash mailman
slash listinfo slash utilities-announce.

00:17:47.399 --> 00:17:55.299
You are not going to get spammed, only updates.

00:17:55.299 --> 00:17:57.200
We try to respect your time and your inbox.

00:17:57.200 --> 00:18:01.210
If it's important we will send things there.

00:18:01.210 --> 00:18:05.149
If you have questions please write to info@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov.

00:18:05.149 --> 00:18:10.989
Also I'll invite you to ask us about negotiating
higher rate keys than 10.

00:18:10.989 --> 00:18:17.529
As we have talked about in previous webinars,
if you think you will need a higher rate than

00:18:17.529 --> 00:18:23.850
10 per second, we can talk with you about
adjusting it upward.

00:18:23.850 --> 00:18:29.230
If you would like to engage us in such a conversation,
we are happy to do that, just write to info

00:18:29.230 --> 00:18:31.190
and we can follow up.

00:18:31.190 --> 00:18:32.789
All right.

00:18:32.789 --> 00:18:34.230
That's what I had to say today.

00:18:34.230 --> 00:18:37.639
I will turn it over to Peter Cooperto see
what questions we have.

00:18:37.639 --> 00:18:38.960
[Peter] Thank you Eric.

00:18:38.960 --> 00:18:40.309
There are a number of questions.

00:18:40.309 --> 00:18:44.350
Some I have addressed and some of them I don't
know the answer to.

00:18:44.350 --> 00:18:47.580
I will let you take a shot.

00:18:47.580 --> 00:18:53.570
There is a question, the first one actually
is about this question of reset time.

00:18:53.570 --> 00:18:58.809
Talking about a particular thing called the
X rate limit reset time, indicating when the

00:18:58.809 --> 00:19:00.669
current window ends.

00:19:00.669 --> 00:19:04.279
The window is one second so I don't quite
know I understand the question.

00:19:04.279 --> 00:19:06.000
Can you address that?

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:10.429
[Eric] I am not sure I would understand the
full context there.

00:19:10.429 --> 00:19:14.960
If you want to talk to us more about that,
just write to info and we will talk to you

00:19:14.960 --> 00:19:15.960
more.

00:19:15.960 --> 00:19:20.590
What I can say is the way, again we determine
the rates, is based on the moving one second

00:19:20.590 --> 00:19:22.630
window.

00:19:22.630 --> 00:19:30.269
We monitor the key or the IP address, and
if we see incoming requests in a given one

00:19:30.269 --> 00:19:34.899
second window that exceeds the rate limit,
if your rate is three and we see more than

00:19:34.899 --> 00:19:42.600
three request starts in the one second window,
then you will get an error.

00:19:42.600 --> 00:19:48.519
Until that rate in the moving one second window
drops below three.

00:19:48.519 --> 00:19:49.830
That is basically what I can say.

00:19:49.830 --> 00:19:53.580
If you have other questions about that, email
and we can follow up.

00:19:53.580 --> 00:19:55.440
[Peter] A couple of questions.

00:19:55.440 --> 00:20:00.679
You addressed them in the talk but I thought
it would be worth pointing out again.

00:20:00.679 --> 00:20:03.350
Somebody did ask about the email and tool
parameter.

00:20:03.350 --> 00:20:07.499
It's not required but very important that
you include that.

00:20:07.499 --> 00:20:09.879
So we can get in touch with you.

00:20:09.879 --> 00:20:13.309
Another one, are the response rates given
the headers?

00:20:13.309 --> 00:20:17.039
I think we discovered that they are given
in the headers.

00:20:17.039 --> 00:20:19.789
[Eric] Thank you for pointing that out that
I should've mentioned it.

00:20:19.789 --> 00:20:23.529
You should be expecting status 429.

00:20:23.529 --> 00:20:29.710
Any error you get should give you a 429, that's
what we have found in our own test.

00:20:29.710 --> 00:20:34.090
We are pretty confident that people are seeing
those.

00:20:34.090 --> 00:20:36.970
It's a little snippet in the header.

00:20:36.970 --> 00:20:40.029
You should be able to look for a 429.

00:20:40.029 --> 00:20:44.289
And the rate is in the error message.

00:20:44.289 --> 00:20:50.779
The integer you will see at the end of that
data blob you get back is the rate that we

00:20:50.779 --> 00:20:52.389
observed.

00:20:52.389 --> 00:20:56.750
On the slide it was 11 I believe, like 1 more
than 10.

00:20:56.750 --> 00:20:58.220
That's what we observed.

00:20:58.220 --> 00:21:05.389
[Peter] Another good question is about many
of the different Perl modules available.

00:21:05.389 --> 00:21:10.429
There is one that he cites, a bio eutilities,
bio db utilities.

00:21:10.429 --> 00:21:16.190
He says it hasn't been updated to support
the API keys.

00:21:16.190 --> 00:21:18.519
Have we reached out to the developers.

00:21:18.519 --> 00:21:19.759
I don't think we have.

00:21:19.759 --> 00:21:22.059
[Eric] Thank you for the question.

00:21:22.059 --> 00:21:23.090
A good point.

00:21:23.090 --> 00:21:31.820
I don't know that we have specifically reached
out to organizations like that.

00:21:31.820 --> 00:21:35.559
We often are in contact with them in any case.

00:21:35.559 --> 00:21:41.759
It's a question honestly for them in terms
of what they would like to support.

00:21:41.759 --> 00:21:46.360
Certainly one approach they could do is have
more support for keys in there and they may

00:21:46.360 --> 00:21:47.710
choose to do that.

00:21:47.710 --> 00:21:52.809
They may choose to have developers solve that
problem on their own.

00:21:52.809 --> 00:21:56.210
I don't have any direct information from them,
but thank you for that.

00:21:56.210 --> 00:22:00.229
It is certainly possible we can get in touch
with them for clarity.

00:22:00.229 --> 00:22:03.940
[Peter] Okay, another question, somebody asked
can I make sure no key will be used for distributed

00:22:03.940 --> 00:22:04.940
service.

00:22:04.940 --> 00:22:10.380
I think the answer is we can't be sure it
won't be used that way.

00:22:10.380 --> 00:22:13.639
Every user on a distrubuted service should
have their own key.

00:22:13.639 --> 00:22:17.380
[Eric] This is a fairly common class of questions.

00:22:17.380 --> 00:22:25.090
A number of software developers will distribute
their code base.

00:22:25.090 --> 00:22:31.610
Basically if you're distributing software
that way, it is good to have a key, right,

00:22:31.610 --> 00:22:36.840
for the individual customers if you think
the use cases the customers have will drive

00:22:36.840 --> 00:22:40.649
their use over three request for second.

00:22:40.649 --> 00:22:46.190
It really depends on the nature of your software
and the number of requests you tend to fire.

00:22:46.190 --> 00:22:48.979
That a typical customer would tend to fire.

00:22:48.979 --> 00:22:52.559
It's a judgement call on your part.

00:22:52.559 --> 00:22:58.950
If you'd like to talk with us about strategies,
that is a lot of the kind of conversations

00:22:58.950 --> 00:23:01.749
we have been having with software developers.

00:23:01.749 --> 00:23:03.879
We are happy to engage in those.

00:23:03.879 --> 00:23:11.039
One of the things you can think about is perhaps
having a default key, that would cover most

00:23:11.039 --> 00:23:12.929
of your customers.

00:23:12.929 --> 00:23:16.860
We can set a rate that would cover most cases.

00:23:16.860 --> 00:23:20.239
You may have some customers that are very
heavy users.

00:23:20.239 --> 00:23:21.919
And you know that.

00:23:21.919 --> 00:23:25.520
We may be able to give them particular keys.

00:23:25.520 --> 00:23:28.429
These are the kinds of things we can talk
about.

00:23:28.429 --> 00:23:32.729
It really just depends a lot on the nature
of your software, how you distribute it, and

00:23:32.729 --> 00:23:36.009
the manner in which you access the utility
API.

00:23:36.009 --> 00:23:39.350
How many request are you making.

00:23:39.350 --> 00:23:43.470
There are ways to talk to you that can help
you reduce the number of request that you

00:23:43.470 --> 00:23:45.210
need to make.

00:23:45.210 --> 00:23:50.999
That's why having these conversations is important,
to find out what would be helpful for your

00:23:50.999 --> 00:23:51.999
customers.

00:23:51.999 --> 00:23:56.399
[Peter] Here is a somewhat philosophical perhaps
question.

00:23:56.399 --> 00:24:02.359
The API key is only there to help NCBI monitor
the request per second ? It's on our personal

00:24:02.359 --> 00:24:05.889
side to adapt our scripts to handle this request
limitation.

00:24:05.889 --> 00:24:09.620
You want to talk about why we are doing this?

00:24:09.620 --> 00:24:13.340
[Eric] A number of topics there we can talked
about.

00:24:13.340 --> 00:24:17.549
One topic is, why do you want to do the keys
at all.

00:24:17.549 --> 00:24:23.429
It comes back to a general part of our mission,
as the national Library of medicine, providing

00:24:23.429 --> 00:24:26.159
free services to the public.

00:24:26.159 --> 00:24:27.729
Free does not mean unregulated.

00:24:27.729 --> 00:24:35.729
Meaning we have had many cases in the past
where particular small number of users, essentially

00:24:35.729 --> 00:24:41.019
created denial of service attack on our servers
because of heavy use.

00:24:41.019 --> 00:24:45.169
Our mission is to relay the services to the
public so we have to do whatever we can to

00:24:45.169 --> 00:24:48.830
make sure the public has access 24/7.

00:24:48.830 --> 00:24:56.309
That basically means preventing excessively
high use for any of the services.

00:24:56.309 --> 00:24:58.669
Whatever it might be.

00:24:58.669 --> 00:25:02.129
The keys help us do that.

00:25:02.129 --> 00:25:05.210
The keys also help you.

00:25:05.210 --> 00:25:08.779
I understand from some perspectives it might
be hard to see that.

00:25:08.779 --> 00:25:12.070
The keys help you because it helps us make
the services more reliable.

00:25:12.070 --> 00:25:15.399
They are less vulnerable to these kinds of
attacks.

00:25:15.399 --> 00:25:19.049
Once the attacks happen you will not be able
to get to us.

00:25:19.049 --> 00:25:22.630
If you have processes that are automated and
your customers depend on those processes,

00:25:22.630 --> 00:25:26.309
we want it to be up to you.

00:25:26.309 --> 00:25:33.080
Preventing these kinds of spikes, and problematic
activity, allows us to have the services up

00:25:33.080 --> 00:25:34.399
more often than not.

00:25:34.399 --> 00:25:41.200
It also allows us to inform you as developers
about what is happening with your software.

00:25:41.200 --> 00:25:44.830
And to alert you earlier to problems we are
seeing.

00:25:44.830 --> 00:25:53.451
If people are abusing your software, or providing
sets of request to us that are harmful, to

00:25:53.451 --> 00:25:59.059
our ability to provide services to the public,
it helps us help you understand what's happening

00:25:59.059 --> 00:26:01.070
and correct those issues.

00:26:01.070 --> 00:26:08.070
So at the moment, the way we are rolling keys
out, yes, as you point out, it kind of is

00:26:08.070 --> 00:26:11.320
on you to think about what your rates are.

00:26:11.320 --> 00:26:16.519
As we explore this further there may be other
ways that we can modify and extend, perhaps

00:26:16.519 --> 00:26:19.649
strengthen the way the keys work, to make
them more flexible.

00:26:19.649 --> 00:26:23.820
We are certainly interested in input from
the community about how that will happen.

00:26:23.820 --> 00:26:27.609
We have had some discussions about that already.

00:26:27.609 --> 00:26:33.169
So we are very open to having your thoughts
and suggestions about ways in which the services

00:26:33.169 --> 00:26:36.970
can be more adaptable to your needs.

00:26:36.970 --> 00:26:38.429
This is where we are currently.

00:26:38.429 --> 00:26:44.109
[Peter] Another, several questions that I
don't think we will have time to get to all

00:26:44.109 --> 00:26:45.109
of them.

00:26:45.109 --> 00:26:47.359
If we don't, we will answer them in a Q&amp;A
document.

00:26:47.359 --> 00:26:53.730
I will put that on the FTP site and you can
look at those .

00:26:53.730 --> 00:26:56.600
One is about being blocked.

00:26:56.600 --> 00:26:59.669
If we continue to exceed the rate will we
be blocked?

00:26:59.669 --> 00:27:01.759
The answer to that I think is no.

00:27:01.759 --> 00:27:09.029
One of the points is that you can't, we are
throttling you so you can't actually do damage.

00:27:09.029 --> 00:27:15.370
[Eric] It's an important question for those
who are experienced users of the API who may

00:27:15.370 --> 00:27:17.989
have been blocked in the past.

00:27:17.989 --> 00:27:26.330
We do have a policy by which we will block
access to an IP or a set of IPs, if we observe

00:27:26.330 --> 00:27:29.669
extraordinarily high rates.

00:27:29.669 --> 00:27:37.749
And the manifestation of such a block is to
have no ability to reach our servers.

00:27:37.749 --> 00:27:42.729
On the web you will see a black screen that
clearly indicates you have been prevented

00:27:42.729 --> 00:27:48.190
access with instructions for how to proceed
to restore access.

00:27:48.190 --> 00:27:52.679
The API keys have nothing to do with that
process at all.

00:27:52.679 --> 00:27:56.429
The API keys are actually a way to help us
prevent that.

00:27:56.429 --> 00:27:57.669
We don't want to be doing that.

00:27:57.669 --> 00:28:01.259
We want people to have access.

00:28:01.259 --> 00:28:06.509
What happens with the keys as Peter indicated,
as I tried to indicate, is an instantaneous

00:28:06.509 --> 00:28:13.749
and very temporary cessation of service for
a particular IP or API key that is giving

00:28:13.749 --> 00:28:16.460
us a high number of requests.

00:28:16.460 --> 00:28:23.559
The manifestation again, of the rate being
exceeded, because I don't want to use the

00:28:23.559 --> 00:28:29.129
term blocked because you aren't, is simply
those individual request will receive an error

00:28:29.129 --> 00:28:33.460
message until the rate drops below the threshold.

00:28:33.460 --> 00:28:39.979
So you will just see gaps in data, data returns
that are unusually small because all they

00:28:39.979 --> 00:28:42.570
will receive is the error message.

00:28:42.570 --> 00:28:45.710
That's what you will see as a user.

00:28:45.710 --> 00:28:50.989
Or if you are a web user are a user of a third-party
app on top of the API, you won't get data

00:28:50.989 --> 00:28:55.979
back for the periods when the rates are high.

00:28:55.979 --> 00:28:59.809
We don't memorize that, there is no recording
of that activity.

00:28:59.809 --> 00:29:04.049
You will get an error until the rates go down
again.

00:29:04.049 --> 00:29:05.719
There is no blocking happening.

00:29:05.719 --> 00:29:08.499
There is no permanent memory of any of that.

00:29:08.499 --> 00:29:10.519
It's just instantaneous responses.

00:29:10.519 --> 00:29:13.619
[Peter] Okay, that is all we have time for.

00:29:13.619 --> 00:29:18.789
As I said if there's additional questions
we didn't address, and all of the questions

00:29:18.789 --> 00:29:21.379
we did address, we will put in the document.

00:29:21.379 --> 00:29:24.629
I would like to thank everybody for coming
and thank you Eric for presenting.

00:29:24.629 --> 00:29:25.929
We will see you next time.

